Why CC0 (relicensed 2026-07-26, superseding the CC BY-NC-SA choice Stage 1.5
made in docs/adr/0009):

  Everything in the artifact is either CC0 already or original curation this
  project owns outright, so nothing compels a restriction:

    - The OpenFarm crops rescue is CC0-1.0. (An earlier draft of DESIGN.md
      guessed CC BY-SA; Stage 1.2 checked the actual repository and corrected
      it — see docs/adr/0006.)
    - The hand-verified spacing figures are facts, which are not copyrightable;
      their selection and arrangement are this project's own work.
    - The companion/antagonist links, the curated plant records and the curated
      soil-moisture table are all hand-authored here.

  CC BY-NC-SA was only ever held to absorb Plants For A Future, whose terms
  would have propagated. That ingest is no longer planned — Stage 6.0 fills the
  dataset's gaps by curation instead — so the restriction had nothing left to
  anticipate, and it was making the work less useful for no benefit.

ATTRIBUTION (courtesy, not a condition)
---------------------------------------
CC0 asks nothing of you. The sources below are credited because knowing where a
figure came from is what makes it checkable, and per-record provenance is
captured in data/plants.json itself:

  - OpenFarm crops rescue (community      https://github.com/thefullnacho/openfarm-crops-rescue  CC0-1.0
    Wayback Machine recovery — see docs/adr/0006; OpenFarm's own live API
    and repository never published a data dump before shutting down in 2025)
  - Hand-verified spacing table           (RHS, Old Farmer's Almanac, Square Foot
    Gardening Foundation and reproduced SFG charts — cited per figure in
    packages/etl/src/spacing/table.ts; see docs/adr/0007)
  - Companion/antagonist relationships    (hand-curated + OpenFarm-derived,
    evidence-tagged; see packages/etl/src/companions and docs/adr/0008)
  - Curated plant records                 (hand-authored, individually cited;
    see packages/etl/src/curated and docs/adr/0021)
  - Curated soil-moisture table           (hand-authored from general
    horticultural consensus, with a stated reason per row rather than
    citations; see packages/etl/src/moisture)
  - GBIF taxonomic backbone               https://www.gbif.org/     (name resolution
    join key; unreachable from the build environment, so no record currently
    carries a GBIF id — see docs/adr/0005)

A note on the spacing and moisture figures: neither was retrieved by fetching
the cited pages from this build environment, whose egress policy blocks them.
Spacing figures came from web-search snippets of those exact pages; moisture
values were authored from general horticultural knowledge. Both are recorded so
a reviewer with unrestricted network access can check them. Treat the dataset as
guidance for planning a garden, not as an authority.

ILLUSTRATIONS
-------------
Crop illustrations under the app's icon set (app/src/icons/, 162 crop icons
plus one generic fallback, Workplan Stage 4.1) are original work: every icon
is programmatically generated by tools/icons/generate.ts from an in-repo
shape library (tools/icons/archetypes.ts), not adapted from any third-party
icon set, font, or clip-art. They are licensed MIT, the same as the rest of
the code (see /LICENSE), and carry no third-party restrictions. See
docs/icon-style-guide.md for the visual conventions and
docs/adr/0019-icon-set-archetypes-and-resolution.md for why they were built
this way instead of as 162 hand-drawn illustrations.

If a future contributor replaces a placeholder icon with hand-drawn or
commissioned artwork that is not their own original work under MIT-compatible
terms, record its source and licence here.

If you add a new data source, record it here and in /data/README.md — and check
its licence first. CC0 is only honest while every input permits it: ingesting a
share-alike or non-commercial source (Plants For A Future, for instance) would
mean relicensing the affected build, not quietly keeping this notice as it is.
